-- title:
--   Theorem 9.3 — the complete coset of compatible module actions in time $p^{O(a)}\cdot\mathrm{poly}(\mathrm{InSize})$
-- statement:
--   There are a deterministic algorithm and a constant $C$ with the following property. Let $p$ be prime, $Q$ a finite group given by a Cayley table with a generating list $S$, $\theta,\theta':Q\to\mathrm{GL}(a,p)$ representations given by their values on $S$, $\Gamma\le\mathrm{Sym}(\Omega)$ the group generated by given permutations $g_1,\dots,g_s$, and $\beta:\Gamma\to\mathrm{Aut}(Q)$ a homomorphism given by the $\beta(g_i)$. On this input, of length $L=\mathrm{InSize}$, the algorithm halts within
--   $$
--   C\cdot p^{\,C a}\cdot(L+2)^C
--   $$
--   steps and outputs the set of compatible pairs
--   $$
--   \{(\alpha,g)\in\mathrm{GL}(a,p)\times\Gamma:\ \alpha\theta(q)=\theta'(\beta_gq)\alpha\ \ (q\in Q)\}
--   $$
--   as a coset: emptiness if it is empty, and otherwise one compatible pair together with generators of exactly the source stabilizer $\{(\sigma,h):\sigma\theta(q)=\theta(\beta_hq)\sigma\ (q\in Q)\}$.
--
--   In a group-extension step with kernel $A\cong\mathbb F_p^a$, $p^{O(a)}$ is polynomial in $|A|$, so this matches the conjugation actions of two extensions in time polynomial in the group order.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The instance code is `encMACInst`. That $S$ generates $Q$ and that $\theta,\theta',\beta$ are homomorphisms are the promises of Definition 27; other inputs are unconstrained.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - reading: p^O(a) · poly(InSize) → C · p^(C·a) · (L+2)^C steps of one machine, L the code length, C chosen before every parameter; reason: one absolute constant per O(·) and per poly(·)
--   - encoding: a-dimensional F_p-spaces M, M′ and Iso_{F_p}(M, M′) → M = M′ = F_p^a and Iso_{F_p}(M, M′) = GL(a, p); reason: fixing bases of M and M′; the set of compatible pairs corresponds bijectively
--   - encoding: "given on a specified generating set"; "generators of Γ ≤ Sym(Ω) and their images under β" → homomorphisms θ, θ′ : Q →* GL(a,p), β : Γ →* Aut(Q) and a list S with ⟨S⟩ = Q as hypotheses; the code writes θ, θ′ on S and β on the generators of Γ; Q labelled by Fin n, Ω = Fin k; reason: the values on generators determine the homomorphisms; the promise is a hypothesis, not an input check
--   - reading: "output elements keep their matrix components and original elements of Γ" → pairs written as the entries of α and the image list of g; reason: the elements themselves; see Theorem 8.1 on straight-line programs
-- source:
--   Qiao, Zhang, Testing isomorphism of finite groups in time polynomial in the group order, preprint, Nov. 3, 2026 (held version sha256 eb9a3355…), Theorem 9.3 and Definition 27, p.93 (PDF p.97)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HMBinPacking_OutputsWithin
import Definitions.Def_GroupIsoPoly_CosetCorrect
import Definitions.Def_GroupIsoPoly_encAnswer
import Definitions.Def_GroupIsoPoly_encMACInst

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace GroupIsoPoly

/-- **Theorem 9.3 (Module action compatibility).**

Qiao, Zhang, *Testing isomorphism of finite groups in time polynomial in the group order*, preprint,
Nov. 3, 2026 (held version sha256 eb9a3355…), Theorem 9.3, p.93 (PDF p.97): "Given the data of
Definition 27, with n = |Q| and total explicit input length InSize, the complete coset of compatible
pairs can be computed deterministically in time p^O(a) · poly(InSize)"; Definition 27, p.93: "The
answer is represented as a witness times the full source stabilizer in GL(M)×Γ, or by emptiness. The
homomorphism β need not be injective, and output elements keep their matrix components and original
elements of Γ."

There are one deterministic machine `M` and one constant `C` such that for every prime `p`, every
`a`, every finite group `Q` labelled by `Fin n` with a generating list `S`, all representations
`θ, θ′ : Q → GL(a, p)`, every list of generators `g₁, …, g_s` of `Γ ≤ Sym(Fin k)` and every
homomorphism `β : Γ → Aut(Q)`, `M` on the code of this data (length `L`, the values of `θ, θ′, β` on
the generators only) halts within `C · p^(C·a) · (L + 2)^C` steps and outputs emptiness if no pair
`(α, g) ∈ GL(a, p) × Γ` satisfies `α θ(q) = θ′(β_g q) α` for all `q`, and otherwise one such pair and a
list generating exactly the source stabilizer `{(σ, h) : σ θ(q) = θ(β_h q) σ for all q}`.

**Formalization Note.** That `S` generates `Q` and that `θ, θ′, β` are homomorphisms are the promises
of Definition 27 ("given on a specified generating set"). Pairs are output as the entries of `α` and
the images of `g`. -/
theorem theorem_9_3 :
    ∃ M : Turing.TM2ComputableAux Bool Bool, ∃ C : ℕ,
      ∀ (p a n t k s : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (Q : Type) [Group Q] (eQ : Q ≃ Fin n) (S : Fin t → Q),
        Subgroup.closure (Set.range S) = ⊤ →
      ∀ (θ θ' : Q →* GL (Fin a) (ZMod p)) (gens : Fin s → Equiv.Perm (Fin k))
        (β : ↥(gammaOf gens) →* MulAut Q),
        ∃ ans : Option ((GL (Fin a) (ZMod p) × ↥(gammaOf gens)) ×
            List (GL (Fin a) (ZMod p) × ↥(gammaOf gens))),
          CosetCorrect (compatPairs θ θ' β) (compatPairs θ θ β) ans ∧
            HMBinPacking.OutputsWithin M (encMACInst eQ S θ θ' gens β)
              (encAnswer (ans.map fun x => (macNats x.1, x.2.map macNats)))
              (C * p ^ (C * a) * ((encMACInst eQ S θ θ' gens β).length + 2) ^ C) := by sorry

end GroupIsoPoly
